has been a winemaking region for over  3000 years  the phoenicians planted the first  grapevines in pre-roman times  the white palomino grape is the source  of sherry a fortified wine  from herith it's exported all over the  world  the andalusian city celebrates the end  of the grape harvest with a major  festival dedicated to the wine  to start off the festivities the grapes  are pressed by foot  for centuries this was the traditional  way to crush the grapes  for an entire week the city celebrates  the wine that made hereth  world famous sherry  my father worked in a wine cellar when i  was a little boy  i met him there after work i'll never  forget the scent of the bodega  this wine is everything to us i knew  that even as a child  my family owes a lot to the wine because  many of us were barrel makers  one of the local wineries that  cultivates this 3000 year old tradition  is the bodega luis perez  but the winery itself is a newcomer  founded just 12 years ago  luis pereth used to teach viticulture or  wine growing at university  now he puts his ideas into practice in  his own winery  he doesn't produce sherry though instead  he produces red wine which was  widespread in the region up until  200 years ago he's planted over 20 grape  varieties  the climate is ideal here and so is the  chalky soil  which does a good job of storing water  in this rather arid region  we know from past centuries that our  soil here is wonderfully suited to red  wine  varieties i'm very interested in culture  especially the development of wine  culture children and young people  nowadays don't know anything about that  anymore  that's why we hold grape tastings not  the usual wine tastings  the differences you taste in the wines  actually originate in the grapes  themselves  the grapes are harvested by hand and  processed immediately  the vineyard focuses on quality over  quantity  we have small plots of land whenever a  plot is ripe  we harvest it that same day we don't  wait  we harvest  has made it his mission to prove that  hadith can do more than just produce  sherry  it's wonderful i'm happy to take a  second taste though  but most visitors for the sherry  especially during the wine festival  in the traditional bodegas they even  have a chance to taste sherry that's  aged at least 30 years  this winery only produces a very old  cherry  the sherry keeps for long periods which  is why it was developed in the first  place  seafarers brought wine with them to sell  in america  but white wine has only 12 to 13 percent  alcohol  they fortified it to keep it from  spoiling on route  we learned how to distill from the arabs  sherry is also aged in a special way the  young wine stays in the top barrels  the older wine is transferred to the  bottom barrels  the bottles are filled from the lowest  layer then  more wine is poured in at the top there  are no vintages the quality is always  the same  you can't find one like this anywhere  else in the world  i'm a wine connoisseur so i want to  visit a variety of wine cellars  this one only produces aged sherry so  that's different  but it's always good to get to know  something new  sherry will be celebrated here in hedeth  through the end of the year  then in november another location will  be chosen to be next year's  european wine city
 

